.\" Original maux page, MIT, 2026-09-21.
.\" Historical-source decision: no Research UNIX or early BSD counterpart
.\" close enough to derive from -- V7 shutdown(8) warns logged-in users on
.\" a timer, which means nothing on a single-user machine.
.\" Rendered by make man-pages; staged on play image in /usr/man/.
.TH SHUTDOWN 1 "September 21, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
shutdown \- stop maux cleanly
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shutdown
.SH DESCRIPTION
Bring the machine to a stop that is safe to walk away from.
.PP
Every open file is closed first.
This is the reason the command exists.
ProDOS writes a file's directory entry back when the file is closed, so a
file that some program still had open when the power went off exists on
the volume with a length of zero, whatever was written into it.
Resetting or switching off loses that;
.B shutdown
does not.
.PP
The periodic interrupt source is then disarmed, a message is printed, and
the processor stops.
Nothing runs afterwards: the shell is gone, the keyboard does nothing, and
the screen keeps whatever was on it.
.PP
Press Ctrl\-Reset to restart.
.B shutdown
arranges for that reset to be a cold start, so the machine reboots from the
disk rather than dropping into the monitor.
.PP
There are no options and no operands.
An operand is refused, and refusing does not stop the machine.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/SHUTDOWN  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
init in
.IR sh (1),
.IR kill (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
There is no exit status on the normal path, because nothing is left to
return to.
Exit status is 2 for an operand.
.SH BUGS
Buffered output belonging to a program that is still running is lost.
.B shutdown
closes the files the system has open; it cannot reach a buffer inside
another program's own memory, any more than a killed program can flush
one.
A program that exits normally has already flushed.
.PP
There is no
.B \-r
option to reboot without a keystroke.
Rebooting from software needs the machine's ROM readable at the reset
vector, which the language-card rule this system enforces does not allow
from ordinary kernel code; the window belongs to a future
.B bye
command, and the reboot option should arrive with it.
.PP
On a machine with an UltraTerm the card keeps driving the monitor across
the reset, so the rebooting system paints a screen that is not the one
being displayed.
That is true of resetting this system at any moment, not only after
.BR shutdown .
